d optimization studies. Selection criteria include the need for precise control over experimental conditions and the ability to measure impurity levels accurately.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis typically involves:\n1. Designing the experiment with appropriate levels of PRESSURE.\n2. Conducting the experiments and collecting data on IMPURITY levels.\n3. Performing statistical analysis using ANOVA or regression techniques to evaluate the significance of PRESSURE on IMPURITY.\n4. Interpreting the results to make informed decisions regarding process adjustments.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Provides a clear understanding of the impact of PRESSURE on IMPURITY.\n- Facilitates optimization of chemical processes to enhance product quality.\n- Allows for the identification of optimal pressure settings that minimize impurities.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- The results may be influenced by uncontrolled variables, leading to confounding effects.\n- The linearity assumption may not hold true in all cases, necessitating more complex modeling.\n- Requires careful experimental design to ensure valid conclusions.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methodologies include response surface methodology (RSM) for exploring the effects of multiple factors simultaneously and Taguchi methods for robust design. Alternatives may involve using non-linear models if the relationship between PRESSURE and IMPURITY is not adequately captured by linear models.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing this analysis, researchers should ensure precise control of pressure settings and accurate measurement of impurity levels. It is also essential to conduct preliminary studies to identify potential confounding factors that may affect the results.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nStatistical software such as R, SAS, or Minitab can be utilized for conducting the analysis. These tools provide functionalities for regression 